course: dictionary
lesson: 1600
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary lists each meaning in turn, and the sentence around the word tells you which one is meant. «Her cheeks went red in the cold wind» uses cheek as a part of the face. The words around it, cold wind, are the clues.
explain: Cheek also means rude talk to someone you should respect. «Sam said sorry to Mrs Hughes for cheek, and meant it» shows Sam had been rude to a teacher. Ask yourself: does the meaning fit here? A face part does not fit with saying sorry, so the second meaning must be right.
explain: Look at the words near the word. «What a cheek!» is a shocked exclamation, so cheek here means that was rude or bold. Try each meaning in the sentence. The one that makes sense is the one you need.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A word can have several meanings, so always check which one fits the sentence.
fact: Cheeks are the soft sides of your face, below your eyes.
fact: Cheek also means rude talk to someone you should respect, especially in British English.
fact: «What a cheek!» means that was rude or bold.
exercise: «At home Jake's little brother took one look at the bruise on Jake's cheek.» Where is a cheek? | *On the face, below the eyes | Inside a shoe | On the back of the hand | At the top of the head
exercise: Sam said sorry to Mrs Hughes for cheek. What had Sam done? | Kissed her to say hello | *Been rude to a teacher | Pinched Mrs Hughes on the cheek | Hidden her pencil case
exercise: Someone says «What a cheek!» after a boy takes the last biscuit without asking. What does cheek mean here? | That was a kind gift | That was very cold | That biscuit was a lovely cheek colour | *That was rude or bold
test: «A hamster can stuff its cheeks so full of seeds that its head looks twice as wide.» What do the cheek pouches let a hamster do? | Run very fast | *Carry lots of seeds | Hear sounds from far away | Change its colour
test: In Bradford, Hamid's cheeks were pinched by about 20 aunties, but he smiled. Why? | He had won a prize | *They were showing love, even in a squeezy way | He was cold and wanted a hug from a hamster | The wedding was over
exam: «Liam's cheeks went red when the whole class laughed at him.» Why did his cheeks go red? | *Embarrassed | Cold | Hungry | Sleepy
